// Cloudflare deployment for the PUBLIC (snapshot-mode) dashboard. The design is
// docs/PUBLIC-DASHBOARD.md; the cutover runbook is infra/cloudflare/README.md
// and the day-to-day one is docs/OPERATIONS.md, "Public dashboard". The private
// dashboard is unaffected — it is still the same bundle served same-origin by
// runner/viewer over loopback.
//
// This is the design doc's **Open** shape, adopted 2026-09-11: the app is static
// assets and nothing else, and the data is a different hostname —
// `wrathbench-data.shard.page`, the R2 bucket behind its own custom domain, with
// the zone's cache rules carrying the TTLs. There is deliberately no `main`
// here: with no fetch handler there is no Worker invocation anywhere in the read
// path, so a traffic spike is absorbed by the edge cache rather than billed as
// compute, and static asset requests are free and unmetered.
//
// What that costs is the ability to say no to a reader, which is the whole
// point: this shape is public. The gate Worker that fronted the zoneless preview
// (`dashboard/worker/`, a shared `DASHBOARD_PASSWORD`) is deleted, not disabled
// — see docs/PUBLIC-DASHBOARD.md, "The gated interim shape", for what it was and
// why it is history.
//
// Two hostnames, so the bucket carries the project's first and only CORS policy
// (`infra/cloudflare/r2-cors.json`, whose `AllowedOrigins` names the app origin
// below). The SPA is built with `VITE_WRATHBENCH_SNAPSHOT_BASE` pointing at the
// data hostname, so it appends `/v1/...` to that rather than to its own origin.
// Minimap tiles are NOT published: they are Blizzard textures and this shape has
// nothing able to keep a reader out, so whether they go public is an open
// operator decision and the build asks for none (dashboard/src/lib/tiles.ts).
//
// Workers Static Assets rather than Pages: parity today, and Cloudflare raises
// limits on Workers first.
//
// Build before deploying — wrangler uploads `dist` as it finds it, and
// `infra/deploy-dashboard.sh` is the supported way to do both:
//
//   bun ship
//
// The deploying shell needs WRATHBENCH_CF_DEPLOY_TOKEN in `.env` (the script
// passes it to wrangler as CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN). Its scopes are Workers
// Scripts:Edit on the account and Workers Routes:Edit on the `shard.page` zone —
// the route below is a Custom Domain, so the deploy creates and owns the DNS
// record for it. It needs no R2 permission at all: the publisher's key pair is a
// separate credential and the two never meet.
{
  "name": "wrathbench-dashboard",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-25",
  // The app's own hostname. `custom_domain` rather than a route: the Worker IS
  // the origin here, so Cloudflare manages the record and the certificate, and
  // there is no origin server behind it for a route to sit in front of.
  "routes": [
    {
      "pattern": "wrathbench.shard.page",
      "custom_domain": true
    }
  ],
  // One origin, not two. The social card's `og:image` is absolute against the
  // hostname above, so a second `*.workers.dev` address for the same site would
  // serve tags pointing somewhere else — and would be a public URL nobody chose.
  "workers_dev": false,
  "assets": {
    // Relative to this file: `bun run --cwd dashboard build` writes dashboard/dist.
    "directory": "./dist",
    // The SPA owns its routes (/runs, /ladder, /run/<id>, /map, ...), so a deep
    // link or a refresh must serve index.html instead of 404ing on a path that
    // only exists in the client router.
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application"
    // No `binding` and no `run_worker_first`: both name a script that no longer
    // exists. Assets are served directly by the platform.
  },
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
